@* Sign-ins per day, one line per app.

   A RadzenChart here and MetricBars next door is not an inconsistency: this is a CONTINUOUS
   series over a date axis, which is exactly the case the repo reserves charts for, while
   reach-per-app is a top-N ranking, which is the case MetricBars exists for. The server
   sends a dense day grid, so every line shares one category axis and a quiet week draws as a
   flat run of zeroes rather than as a segment joining the two days either side of it.

   Collapsed by default, like the 30-day history on /azure: it is corroborating detail behind
   the roster, and a chart is the most expensive thing on this page to draw. *@
@using PoPunkouterSoftware.Shared
@using Radzen.Blazor

<details class="signin-disclosure">
    <summary class="signin-disclosure__heading">
        <span><RadzenIcon Icon="timeline" /><strong>Sign-ins over time</strong></span>
        <span class="app-note">@WindowDays days, by app</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="signin-disclosure__body">
        @if (Series.Count == 0)
        {
            <div class="signin-empty">No sign-ins in this window.</div>
        }
        else
        {
            <RadzenChart>
                @foreach (var series in Series)
                {
                    <RadzenLineSeries TItem="SignInTrendPoint" Data="@series.Points" Title="@series.App"
                                      CategoryProperty="Day" ValueProperty="SignIns" />
                }
                @* A DATE axis, which is what makes this readable. Bound to strings the axis
                   draws one tick per category and ignores both Step and TickDistance, so 30
                   day-labels ran into each other and 90 were a solid grey band. Given
                   DateTime values it thins the ticks to the available width by itself, which
                   is also why this needs no mobile breakpoint. *@
                <RadzenCategoryAxis FormatString="{0:MMM d}" />
                @* Whole people, not 0.5 of one. Without the format string the axis invents
                   fractional ticks whenever the busiest day is 1, which is most days here. *@
                <RadzenValueAxis FormatString="{0:N0}" />
            </RadzenChart>
        }
    </div>
</details>

@code {
    /// <summary>One dense series per app, busiest first, with "Other" last when present.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<SignInTrendSeries> Series { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>Days covered — shown in the summary line so the chart is not read as all-time.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int WindowDays { get; set; }

}
